[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should the userId come from? → Caller passes it in[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Caller-supplied it is. That settles the signature; the open piece is what the submit handler passes, since nothing on
  the page holds a userId today.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the handler at [38;5;153mapp.js:17-28[39m reads its values from DOM inputs. To pass a userId it needs a value from
  somewhere, and the choices differ in how much of the page changes.

  - [1mNew hidden input in [38;5;153mindex.html[0m — handler reads it like the others, one-line symmetry with [38;5;153musername[39m/[38;5;153mpassword[39m. Costs a
    markup change, and something still has to populate it (server-rendered, or hardcoded for now). Honest about the
    fact that the value comes from outside the form.
  - [1mRead from browser storage / URL[0m — e.g. [38;5;153mlocalStorage.getItem("userId")[39m or a query param. No markup change; costs a
    new implicit dependency on state this app never sets, so it reads as [38;5;153mnull[39m until something else writes it.
  - [1mOptional parameter, caller passes nothing yet[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m with the param trailing and
    defaulting to [38;5;153mnull[39m. Lands the signature and the tracking now, leaves the wiring for when you know the source.
    Cheapest to change later; costs that the feature doesn't actually track anything until the source arrives.

  [1mMy recommendation: the hidden input.[0m It keeps the userId's origin visible in the markup next to the fields it travels
  with, and it's the one option where the handler has a real value to pass on day one. If the userId is genuinely not
  available yet, the third option is the honest placeholder — say so and I'll do that instead.
